  "query_preview": "period. An areawide Heat Advisory will remain in effect\nthrough 8 PM Sunday for heat indices up to 109 F. A small shift\nin the synoptic pattern will come into fruition late Saturday\ninto early Sunday as this week's upper-level ridging begins to\nretrograde over the Plains and weak northerly flow begins to move\nover the eastern Mississippi Valley. A weak backdoor cold front\nand cold air advection will also begin to make its way across the\nMid-South early Sunday. However, without a cold airmass trailing\nbehind this front, we won't see much movement in the way of\ntemperatures, just a wind shift. As far as precipitation goes,\nincreased Gulf moisture in combination with peak daytime heating\nwill allow for a few pop-up showers and storms both this\nafternoon and tomorrow afternoon. Though any precipitation that\ndoes fall will not be appreciable. Past tomorrow, dry air\ntrailing behind the front will decrease moisture across the area\nresulting in little to no precipitation next week.\n\nThe latest ensemble members have this ridge building back east by\nmidday Monday. One thing to note, dry air and cold air advection\nbehind the aforementioned front is expected to decrease dewpoints\nwith many areas seeing readings well into the 60s to potential\nupper 50s Monday and Tuesday. Though the ridge begins to build\nback east, keeping highs in the upper 90s to lower 100s next\nworkweek, heat indices may be able to skirt just below the 105F\nHeat Advisory threshold. With prolonged heat and little to no\novernight relief, heat headlines may still be warranted past\nSunday, but decisions on such will be made in subsequent forecast\npackages as details become more clear. Bottom line, it is going\nto remain hot and dry for at least the next 2 weeks. A worsening\nof drought conditions is expected as this prolonged stent of\nlittle to no precipitation persists. The latest CPC 8-14 day\noutlook has us highlighted in a 70-80% chance for above normal\ntemperatures through mid September.\n\nAEH",
